{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 110 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "78ab77e0326e8b1d2006ebd2d970b92edae383ddcacc808a2d38c2b4644dc285" :edb/db-before-t 109 :edb/db-after-t 110 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533422 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:46:04.450Z" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186059853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326675 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186059853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326676 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186059853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326678 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326677 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186059854 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326679 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186064206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326920 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186064206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326921 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186064759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062581 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186064759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326379 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186064759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186064759 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x =-2$ and $f(-2) = 2$, then what is the value of\n$lim_(x → (-2))((f(x))^{2} + 4)$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186064759 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4)\\,?\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186064759 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4) & =({2}^{2}+4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186068706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326687 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186068706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326697 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186068768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326681 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186068846 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326691 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186068847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326696 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067042 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326429 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069317 1071 "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 2$ and $lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) =-1$, then what is $lim_(x → 2^{+})(f(x) + f(2))$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069317 1071 "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(f(x)+f(2))?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069317 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=2,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=f(2)=-1.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(f(x)+f(2)) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)+f(2) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067048 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326559 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069318 1071 "![](images/18/188e0169738a08b3d4580b4f5d3a3ab1aedef87a9d1b83f07c1b10686a38c321.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y = g(x)$ above?\n\n1. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$\n2. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$\n3. The function is continuous at $x = 0$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069318 1071 "![](images/18/188e0169738a08b3d4580b4f5d3a3ab1aedef87a9d1b83f07c1b10686a38c321.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=g(x)$ above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069318 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x)=0\\ne -2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x)=-2=g(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067054 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326435 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069319 1071 "![](images/89/89299bca567844fbc51f0bc245acea2b62e6c0de387e18b6052a6608605c0b48.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y = f(x)$ above?\n\n1. The function is left-continuous at $x =-1$\n2. The function is right-continuous at $x =-1$\n3. The function is continuous at $x =-1$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069319 1071 "![](images/89/89299bca567844fbc51f0bc245acea2b62e6c0de387e18b6052a6608605c0b48.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true in relation to the function $y=f(x)$ above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=-1$\n- The function is continuous at $x=-1$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069319 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=-1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=3\\ne 2=f(-1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=-1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067084 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326528 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069324 1071 "![](images/01/01b742fa8f4bf42597e8653cf6b379537d95c3789743a564064a768ca343d197.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y = \\frac{a}{x} + k$. What is the value of $a + k$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069324 1071 "![](images/01/01b742fa8f4bf42597e8653cf6b379537d95c3789743a564064a768ca343d197.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y=\\frac{a}{x}+k.$ What is the value of $a+k?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069324 1074 "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=1.$ This means that $k=1$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,2.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x}+1 \\\\ 2.5 & =\\frac{a}{1}+1 \\\\ 1.5 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+k=1+1.5=2.5.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067150 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326320 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069335 1071 "Given that $(6y)/(5(1 - 2y)) = p$ and $\\frac{9 - 4y}{1 - 2y} = q$, what is $p + q$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069335 1071 "Given that $\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}=p$ and $\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y}=q,$ what is $p+q?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069335 1074 "We wish to add the following fractions:\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $5(1-2y)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{9-4y}{1-2y} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{5}{5}\\cdot \\frac{9-4y}{1-2y} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y}{5(1-2y)}+\\frac{5(9-4y)}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y+5(9-4y)}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6y+45-20y}{5(1-2y)} & = \\\\ \\frac{-14y+45}{5(1-2y)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067156 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326232 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069336 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{5 + b}{3b} + \\frac{6 - b}{3b}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069336 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{5+b}{3b}+\\frac{6-b}{3b}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069336 1074 "We have a common denominator of $3b,$ so we add the numerators and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5+b}{3b}+\\frac{6-b}{3b} & = \\\\ \\frac{5+b+6-b}{3b} & = \\\\ \\frac{(5+6)+(b-b)}{3b} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{3b}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067192 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326626 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069342 1071 "The line $y = 2x + 4$ intersects the hyperbola $y =-\\frac{k + 1}{x} + 1$, where $k \\ne - 1$, only once. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186069342 1071 "The line $y=2x+4$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1$, where $k\\ne -1$, only once. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186069342 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=2x+4$ intersects the hyperbola $y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1$,  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x+4 &  \\\\ y=-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & =-\\frac{k+1}{x}+1 \\\\ 2x+3 & =-\\frac{k+1}{x} \\\\ (2x+3)x & =-(k+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3x+k+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(2)(k+1)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-8k-8}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{1-8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=\\frac{1}{8}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186078440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326766 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186078498 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326862 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186078499 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326831 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186078503 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326864 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186078554 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326761 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083242 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326739 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326820 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326833 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326682 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326685 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326745 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326803 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186081023 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186083687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326357 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083687 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x) = 3x^{3} + 5x^{2} - 15x - 25$ has a root in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$, find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x)$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186083687 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-15x-25$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083687 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $3$ | $5$ | $-15$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $25$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186081029 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186083688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326650 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083688 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x) = x^{3} - 7x^{2} + 18x - 18$ has a root in the interval $(2, 7)$, find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186083688 1071 "Given that the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+18x-18$ has a root in the interval $(2,7),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186083688 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-18$ and the leading coefficient is $1.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-18}{\\text{factor of }1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 9,\\,\\pm 18.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(2,7),$ are $3$ and $6.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$3$\n$1$\n$-7$\n$18$\n$-18$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$3$\n$-12$\n$18$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$-4$\n$6$\n$0$\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-3)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)({x}^{2}-4x+6).\n$$\n\nNow we must see if the polynomial $g(x)={x}^{2}-4x+6$ has real roots. For this, we must find the value of its discriminant $D.$ Note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-4{)}^{2}-4(1)(6) \\\\  & =16-24 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0,$ the polynomial $g(x)$ doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=3.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186087768 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326850 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186087848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326789 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186087849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326790 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186087849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326797 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186087851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326798 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186087859 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326809 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085620 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186088166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326396 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088166 1071 "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T: R^{2} → R^{3}$, find $T(v)$.\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186088166 1071 "Consider the matrix $T$ and the vector $v$ given below. If $T$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ find $T(v).$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088166 1074 "Using the fact that $\\,T(v)=T\\cdot v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =T\\cdot v \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085626 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186088167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326300 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088167 1071 "$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}ay+2x-2z \\\\ bz-3x+cy\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088167 1071 "$T = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}ay + 2x - 2z \\\\ bz - 3x + cy\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T: R^{3} → R^{2}$ shown above. What is the value of $a + b + c$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186088167 1074 "Given the vector $\\,[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-3y-2z \\\\ -3x-y+4z\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3y+2x-2z \\\\ 4z-3x-y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3y+2x-2z \\\\ 4z-3x+(-1)y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b+c=4+(-3)+(-1)=0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085632 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186088168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326295 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088168 1071 "$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -8 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x+y \\\\ ax-8y \\\\ -9x+by\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ shown above. What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088168 1071 "$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -8 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-3x + y \\\\ ax - 8y \\\\ -9x + by\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the standard matrix $T$ of the linear transformation $T: R^{2} → R^{3}$ shown above. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186088168 1074 "Given the vector $\\,[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -8 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3x+y \\\\ -2x-8y \\\\ -9x+7y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x+y \\\\ -2x-8y \\\\ -9x+7y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+b=-2+7=5.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085638 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186088169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326330 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088169 1071 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{5}{x^{3} + x^{2} + x}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186088169 1071 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186088169 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{x({x}^{2}+x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $2x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326887 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326890 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092227 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326907 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326908 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092357 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326883 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092357 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326884 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092358 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326882 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092367 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326777 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092367 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326778 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092368 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326779 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090358 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186092698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326566 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092698 1071 "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $75$, and the common ratio is $5$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1713$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186092698 1071 "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $75$, and the common ratio is $5.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,713?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092698 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=75$ and  $r=5$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{3-1} \\\\ 75 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\ 75 & =25{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,713.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{n-1} & >1\\,713 \\\\ {5}^{n-1} & >571 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{5}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(571) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(571) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(571)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}+1≈4.944\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,713.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090364 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186092699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326471 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092699 1071 "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$2020$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186092699 1071 "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$2\\,020?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092699 1074 "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=5$ and ${a}_{6}=40$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=5$ and  $r=2$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{3-1} \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,020.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{n-3} & >2\\,020 \\\\ {2}^{n-3} & >404 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{2}^{n-3} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3≈11.658\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,020.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090412 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186092707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326563 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092707 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r = 5$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186092707 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=5?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092707 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation $r=5$ is a circle of radius $5$ centered at the origin. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/4c/4cbe81e57ef23a74ecbafbaf17edf84eff038d1e44253a90689cf5154d3b5a27.png)" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186090418 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186092708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326431 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186092708 1074 "Notice that the given curve is a circle of radius $6$ centered at the origin.\n\nTherefore, its polar equation is $r=6.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186096763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326878 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186100882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326922 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186100882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326924 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186100882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326925 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186100900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326815 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186101407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099573 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186101407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326616 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186101407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186101407 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}$ that satisfies $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186101407 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2x}{y^{2}}$ that satisfies $y(2) = \\sqrt[3]{13}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186101407 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3} & ={x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =3{x}^{2}+3{C}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}$  is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{13})}^{3} & =3(2)^{2}+C \\\\ 13 & =12+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=\\sqrt[3]{13}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}=3{x}^{2}+1.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186105913 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326825 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186105913 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326932 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186105914 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326931 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186111174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186108739 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186111174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326643 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186111174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186111174 1071 "What is the graph of $y = \\arcsin x$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186111174 1071 "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186111174 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n- Its minimum value occurs at $x=-1.$\n\n- Its maximum value occurs at $x=1.$\n\n- Its zero occurs at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115207 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326841 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115207 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326854 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115207 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326885 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115207 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326886 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326926 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115233 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326927 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326930 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326936 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115340 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326937 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115341 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326938 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113899 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186115832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326264 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115832 1071 "Determine the range of the function $f(x) = 2\\log_{5} (2x - 1) + 1$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186115832 1071 "Determine the range of the function $f(x)=2{log}_{5}⁡(2x-1)+1.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115832 1074 "The range of the function $y={log}_{5}⁡(x)$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nHorizontal and vertical shifts and stretches do not affect the range of a logarithmic function.\n\nTherefore, the range of the given function is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113935 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186115838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326427 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115838 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-3y+z=0 \\\\  & -4x+6y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115838 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n${x - 3y + z = 0; - 4x + 6y = 2$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The system is consistent dependent\n2. The system has no solution\n3. The system is consistent independent" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186115838 1074 "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we can to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113947 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186115840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326397 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115840 1071 "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186115840 1071 "Which of the following matrices are block diagonal?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186115840 1074 "A block diagonal matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and all the other blocks contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block diagonal matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block diagonal matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186119780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326933 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186119788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326849 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186119790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326935 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118640 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186120426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326534 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120426 1071 "Given that $det(A) = det(B)$, which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B$, where\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186120426 1071 "Given that $det(A)=det(B),$ which of the following elementary row operations transforms matrix $A$ to matrix $B,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120426 1074 "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118652 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186120428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326362 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120428 1071 "Consider the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is upper triangular\n2. $A$ is lower triangular\n3. $A^{T}$ is upper triangular" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186120428 1071 "Consider the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is upper triangular\n- $A$ is lower triangular\n- ${A}^{T}$ is upper triangular" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120428 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $A$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. All the entries above the main diagonal of $A$ are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. All the entries below the main diagonal of ${A}^{T}$ are zeros:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118664 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186120430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326392 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120430 1071 "Calculate\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186120430 1071 "Calculate\n$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186120430 1074 "The $3$rd row of the matrix contains two zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot {C}_{31}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+1\\cdot {C}_{34} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot {C}_{31}+2\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{33} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-2)-2(2-4) \\\\  & =-1+4 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the second $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+(-4)\\cdot {C}_{22}+(-2)\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4(2+3)+2(1+6) \\\\  & =-20+14 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|=2\\cdot 3-(-6)=12.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124523 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326819 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124525 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326708 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124525 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326710 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124527 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326733 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124527 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326734 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124542 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326714 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124542 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326817 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124542 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326818 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124544 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326715 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124545 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326735 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124545 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326736 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124546 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326731 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124548 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326729 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124558 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326713 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124582 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326732 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124609 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326695 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186124609 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326730 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123338 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186125128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326459 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125128 1071 "Let $B$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and let $T: R^{2} → R^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T]_{B}$, given that\n\n$T(b_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$, $T(b_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, and $B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186125128 1071 "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,$ $T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125128 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123368 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186125133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326463 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125133 1071 "Su