nd $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125144 1074 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x,$ $y,$ and $z.$ From the third equation, we obtain $z=0$ and from the second equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is ${[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123440 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186125145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326316 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186125145 1071 "Given that $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186125145 1071 "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 7 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{aligned}],