7297 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326493 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129641 1071 "A metal plate occupies the region $D = {(x, y): - 1 \\le y \\le 1, 0 \\le x \\le y^{2}}$. The charge density of the plate is given by $ρ(x, y) = xy^{2}$. Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129641 1071 "A metal plate occupies the region $D={(x,y):-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$ The charge density of the plate is given by $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ Find the total charge $Q$ of the plate." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129641 1074 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/1c/1cbe96aa4bf517302a2329db9f8eb2e9c56873e4d5a18cd5f1cd676543e97086.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region. So, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}-0)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{7}}{7}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7}-(-\\frac{{1}^{7}}{7})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{1}{7}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127309 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326335 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129643 1071 "For the first year of a newborn's life, its height $H$ increases at a rate that is proportional to the time, in months, since birth.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height of the baby at time $t$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129643 1071 "For the first year of a newborn's life, its height $H$ increases at a rate that is proportional to the time, in months, since birth.\nWhat differential equation describes the rate of change of the height of the baby at time $t?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129643 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*For the first year of a newborn's life, its **height** $H$ **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **time**, in months, since birth.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *time* since birth. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127321 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326598 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129645 1071 "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129645 1071 "$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}, P_{B → C} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{B → C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129645 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127333 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326555 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129647 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129647 1071 "$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}, [T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129647 1074 "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127351 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326587 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129650 1071 "If $Re(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$ and $Im(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}e & f \\\\ g & h\\end{bmatrix}$ are the real and imaginary parts of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 + 5\\textrm{i} & -2 - 4\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 - 3\\textrm{i} & 1 - 6\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $ad + eh$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129650 1071 "If $\\,\\text{Re}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\text{Im}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}e & f \\\\ g & h\\end{aligned}]\\,$ are the real and imaginary parts of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3+5\\text{i} & -2-4\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i} & 1-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ then what is the value of $ad+eh?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129650 1074 "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3+5\\text{i} & -2-4\\text{i} \\\\ 2-3\\text{i} & 1-6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=3,d=1,e=5$ and $h=-6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nad+eh=3\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-6)=-27.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127357 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326494 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129651 1071 "If $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 - \\textrm{i} & 2 - 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 - 6\\textrm{i} & 3 - 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $x = \\begin{bmatrix}5 - 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$, then $Ax =$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129651 1071 "If $\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & 2-2\\text{i} \\\\ 3-6\\text{i} & 3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\,$, then $\\,Ax=$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129651 1074 "Multiplying the matrix by the vector using the usual method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & 2-2\\text{i} \\\\ 3-6\\text{i} & 3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(1-\\text{i})(5-2\\text{i})+(2-2\\text{i})(2+\\text{i}) \\\\ (3-6\\text{i})(5-2\\text{i})+(3-3\\text{i})(2+\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(3-7\\text{i})+(6-2\\text{i}) \\\\ (3-36\\text{i})+(9-3\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\text{i} \\\\ 12-39\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127369 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326340 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129653 1071 "Find $B = A^(―)$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 + 4\\textrm{i} & 3 - 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 - 3\\textrm{i} & 1 - 5\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$. What is the value of $b_{11} + b_{22}$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129653 1071 "Find $B=\\overset{―}{A}$, where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} & 3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 3-3\\text{i} & 1-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].$ What is the value of ${b}_{11}+{b}_{22}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129653 1074 "Taking the complex conjugate of each entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{A} & =\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} & 3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 3-3\\text{i} & 1-5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{2+4\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{3-2\\text{i}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{3-3\\text{i}} & \\overset{―}{1-5\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 3+3\\text{i} & 1+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${b}_{11}+{b}_{22}=(2-4\\text{i})+(1+5\\text{i})=3+\\text{i}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127375 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326393 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129654 1071 "Find the real part of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 + 3\\textrm{i} & -5 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 6 - 7\\textrm{i} & 4 - 9\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129654 1071 "Find the real part of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & -5+2\\text{i} \\\\ 6-7\\text{i} & 4-9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129654 1074 "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & -5+2\\text{i} \\\\ 6-7\\text{i} & 4-9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & -9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Re}(A)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127435 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326465 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129664 1071 "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129664 1071 "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129664 1074 "The scale factor $r$ of the rotation-scaling matrix $[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}]$ can be found using the formula \n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{det(A)}=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127441 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326634 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129665 1071 "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129665 1071 "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $C = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129665 1074 "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127459 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326315 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129668 1071 "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3+4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129668 1071 "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 3 + 4\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A = PCP^{-1}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129668 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127471 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326479 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129670 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129670 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A = PCP^{-1}$, where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of\n$\\frac{a^{2} + b^{2}}{c^{2} + d^{2}}$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129670 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +13 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-2\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+3\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127507 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326289 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129676 1071 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ about the perpendicular line passing through its center of mass is $0.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nIf the moment of inertia of the same lamina about another perpendicular line is $0.25\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ find the distance between the two lines. Round your final answer to two decimal places." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129676 1071 "The moment of inertia of a plane lamina of mass $0.5kg$ about the perpendicular line passing through its center of mass is $0.2kg \\cdot m^{2}$.\nIf the moment of inertia of the same lamina about another perpendicular line is $0.25kg \\cdot m^{2}$, find the distance between the two lines. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129676 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nApplying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 0.25 & =0.2+0.5{d}^{2} \\\\ 0.5{d}^{2} & =0.05 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =0.1 \\\\ d & =0.316\\,22….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the two axes is $0.32\\,m,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127525 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326597 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129679 1071 "Peter has planted a tree. The height $h$ of the tree increases at a rate that is proportional to the height of the tree. What differential equation describes the rate of growth of the height of the tree at time $t$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129679 1071 "Peter has planted a tree. The height $h$ of the tree increases at a rate that is proportional to the height of the tree. What differential equation describes the rate of growth of the height of the tree at time $t?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129679 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **height** of the tree **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **height** of the tree.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *height* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *height.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}∝h.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the height *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=kh.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127531 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326436 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129680 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129680 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}f(x, y)dydx + \\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4 - x}f(x, y)dydx$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129680 1074 "![](images/ea/ea36f1ec1ddeb03dd8d133fb8d65b5d50b3fa07ba34495978d87f95742dee042.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-x\\,⟹\\,x=4-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e8/e82098a4bb63f1d709a06b6a24da078594716d6a3cc0fa2e069af9dcd9154955.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127561 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326604 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129685 1071 "![](images/fb/fb4959f437e11b767be136a2a024260d45ed29805cd57b1832d74d7cc2da11c1.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $y = P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $P(x) → ∞$ as $x → - ∞$.\n2. The degree of $P(x)$ is odd.\n3. The coefficient of the leading term is negative." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129685 1071 "![](images/fb/fb4959f437e11b767be136a2a024260d45ed29805cd57b1832d74d7cc2da11c1.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n- The degree of $P(x)$ is odd.\n- The coefficient of the leading term is negative." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129685 1074 "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127573 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326231 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129687 1071 "![](images/8f/8f21144219a628cbaf96d7ca052e14dd2e619c4776d49208cdd42305fc51180b.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y = x(x + 1)(x - 1)$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129687 1071 "![](images/8f/8f21144219a628cbaf96d7ca052e14dd2e619c4776d49208cdd42305fc51180b.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=x(x+1)(x-1)?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129687 1074 "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0,\\pm 1$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at these points. Moreover, because $x=0,\\pm 1$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at these points.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{II}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is positive, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $\\infty .$ Since only graph $\\text{IV}$ displays this end behavior, the correct answer is $\\text{IV}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127579 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326543 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129688 1071 "![](images/10/10af2af004ec61307d14a56f61a4a14acc87ded9ed38c3168c6b165b412eb5f0.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y =-5x^{2}(x - 1)^{2}(x - 2)$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129688 1071 "![](images/10/10af2af004ec61307d14a56f61a4a14acc87ded9ed38c3168c6b165b412eb5f0.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=-5{x}^{2}(x-1{)}^{2}(x-2)?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129688 1074 "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=2$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0,1$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at these points.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{IV}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{IV}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127597 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326292 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129691 1071 "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 + 7\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-1 - 2\\textrm{i}$ and $λ_{3} =-2$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129691 1071 "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129691 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127615 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326497 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129694 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129694 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129694 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127633 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326327 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129697 1074 "First, we notice that the function has precisely three distinct roots, $x=-3,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=2.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=2,$ this root is a simple root (the multiplicity equals $1$).\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=(x+3)(x+1{)}^{2}(x-2).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127651 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326455 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129700 1071 "What is the range of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{2x + 3} + 1$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129700 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2x+3}+1?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129700 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=1.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5cded0bfa8f969d9e6a11092ffbe22196e0d987a79c9d7812a4d3a8cde2f5c29.png)" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127657 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326556 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129701 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x) = \\frac{1}{3x + 9} - 3$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129701 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3x+9}-3?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129701 1074 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+9 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (-3,\\infty ).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127735 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326467 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129714 1071 "Given the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\textrm{i} - 1 & 0 \\\\ 2\\textrm{i} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following elementary row operations:\n$R_{1} = \\frac{1}{2\\textrm{i} - 1}R_{1}, R_{2} = R_{2} - 2\\textrm{i}R_{1}$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129714 1071 "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2\\text{i} & 1\\end{aligned}]$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following  elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}-1}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}={R}_{2}-2\\text{i}{R}_{1}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129714 1074 "Let's apply the row operations to $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2\\text{i} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}-1}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2\\text{i} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2\\text{i}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the result is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127765 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326523 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129719 1071 "Given that $(x + 3)$ is a factor of $f(x) = 2x^{3} + 8x^{2} + 7x + 3$, which of the following curves is generated by $y = f(x)$?\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129719 1071 "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+7x+3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129719 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 8 & 7 & 3 \\\\  &  & -6 & -6 & -3 \\\\  & 2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+3}=2{x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+3)(2{x}^{2}+2x+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(1)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)^{3}+8(0)^{2}+7(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png)" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127789 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326239 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129723 1071 "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1+6\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186129723 1071 "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 + 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 1 + 6\\textrm{i}$, find a matrix $P$ such that $A = PCP^{-1}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186129723 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & 