rals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{3}-2⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}\\cdot ({\\pi }^{3}-3)-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131527 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326588 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134253 1071 "Find $H^{⊥}$ if $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134253 1071 "Find ${H}^{⊥}$ if $H=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134253 1074 "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}$ and ${h}_{2}.$ \n\nA vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0$ and $x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {h}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =