ven that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134268 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}},$ $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}},$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{3}}{{a}_{3}\\cdot {a}_{3}}{a}_{3}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+4\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1}{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{11}{12}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(21[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+11[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131629 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326310 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134270 1071 "Given that $‖4u + v‖ = 22$ and $‖v‖ = 2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134270 1071 "Given that $‖4u+v‖=22$ and $‖v‖=2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134270 1074 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le ‖4u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le 4‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 22 & \\le 4‖u‖+2 \\\\ 20 & \\le 4‖u‖ \\\\ 5 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $5.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131653 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326363 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134274 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$∭_(R)2ydV$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (y, z) ∈ D, 0 \\le x \\le z\\sqrt{3}}$,\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axes, the line $y = 2$ and the line $z = y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134274 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,0\\le x\\le z\\sqrt{3}},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $yz$-plane enclosed between the $y$-axes, the line $y=2$ and the line $z=y$ in the first quadrant." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134274 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2y\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z\\sqrt{3}}2y\\,dx]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z\\sqrt{3}}2y\\,dx]dA & ={∬}_{D}2y[x]_{x=0}^{x=z\\sqrt{3}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate the double integral above over the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/63/63a8768b2769fad339ba7667357812d2dda0ea856e846ad666a100f8bc1336e9.png)\n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}2yz\\sqrt{3}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}2z\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=y}dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\sqrt{3}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{16}{4}-0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131671 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326353 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134277 1071 "![](images/bc/bc1b0ea463b0f753a2c65784fa5d46203cde8f457116c481301bf3f20ce82f54.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)4ydV$\nover the region $R$, shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134277 1071 "![](images/bc/bc1b0ea463b0f753a2c65784fa5d46203cde8f457116c481301bf3f20ce82f54.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n${∭}_{R}4y\\,dV$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134277 1074 "![](images/9b/9bb9311cbaf796f76c4b8b7cb7ed81abec48abc445b894f838f940fbd92e534b.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y-z},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/82/82f237dc5fb7d8b8ed34b7a1c16527ab338923a33462f0bd7dafbe54bc5e5158.png)\n\nHence, we can express the region $R$ as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-y-z}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4y\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-y-z}4y\\,dx]dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4y[x]_{x=0}^{x=4-y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4y(4-y-z)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16y-4{y}^{2}-4yz)\\,dz]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[16yz-4{y}^{2}z-2y{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(14y-4{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(14y-4{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={[7{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =(28-\\frac{32}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131683 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326444 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134279 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}}$ where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is orthogonal. Round the answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134279 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134279 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-1)}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,9\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 11+(-1)\\cdot (-7)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{11}^{2}+(-7)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{3}\\sqrt{171}} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{57}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{57}}{9})≈{32.98}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131695 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326277 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134281 1071 "Given that $‖7u + v‖ = 32$ and $‖v‖ = 4$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134281 1071 "Given that $‖7u+v‖=32$ and $‖v‖=4$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134281 1074 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖7u+v‖ & \\le ‖7u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖7u+v‖ & \\le 7‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 32 & \\le 7‖u‖+4 \\\\ 28 & \\le 7‖u‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $4.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131701 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326533 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134282 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}f(x, y)ds$, where $f(x, y) = \\sqrt{2}(x - y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized\nby $r(t) = ⟨t^{2} + t, t^{2} - t⟩$ for $t ∈ [0, \\sqrt{2}]$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134282 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized \n by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\sqrt{2}]?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134282 1074 "Since $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,{t}^{2}-t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}+t,$ and $y={t}^{2}-t.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t+1)^{2}+(2t-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4t+1+4{t}^{2}-4t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2}(x-y)$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2}[({t}^{2}+t)-({t}^{2}-t)] \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2\\sqrt{2}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131713 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326655 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134284 1071 "Given the polynomial $P(x) = (x + 2)(x - 1)(x - 2)$, find the solution of $P(x) > 0$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134284 1071 "Given the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2),$ find the solution of $P(x)>0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134284 1074 "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2)$ are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm 2,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/65/65a899ed4375be4681c0e4598cc4769335cabb52257a886148438f4b4f16e671.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-2,1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131719 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326285 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134285 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^{3} - 4x \\le 8 - 2x^{2}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134285 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}-4x\\le 8-2{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134285 1074 "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4x & \\le 8-2{x}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{3}-4x)-(8-2{x}^{2}) & \\le 0 \\\\ ({x}^{3}-4x)+(2{x}^{2}-8) & \\le 0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4)+2({x}^{2}-4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+2)({x}^{2}-4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-2)(x+2) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}(x-2) & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)^{2}(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,2),\\,(2,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(x+2)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131725 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326570 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134286 1071 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x = [x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}]^{T}$, extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of $R^{3}$. What is the value of $\\frac{x_{1}}{x_{2}}$?\n\n$U = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134286 1071 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134286 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above, which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131743 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326284 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134289 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134289 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n1. $U = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n2. $V = {\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}$\n3. $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134289 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=1+0=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+1=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0=0\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131749 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326606 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134290 1071 "Solve the inequality $x^{4} - 1 < 0$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134290 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}-1<0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134290 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(x+1)(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)(x-1)({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(-1,1)$ only.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-1,1).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131755 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326481 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134291 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}12z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134291 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$∬_(D)[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{y}12zdx]dA$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $yz$-plane is given by\n$D = {(y, z): 0 \\le y \\le 2, 0 \\le z \\le 4 - y^{2}}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134291 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}12z\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}12z[x]_{x=0}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12z(y-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12yz\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}yz\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}12yz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6y[{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=4-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6y(4-{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(96y-48{y}^{3}+6{y}^{5})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[48{y}^{2}-12{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =48(2)^{2}-12(2)^{4}+(2)^{6} \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131767 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326525 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134293 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2xyz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-2z},\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134293 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$∬_(D)[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}2xyzdy]dA$\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n$D = {(x, z): 0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{4 - 2z}, 0 \\le z \\le 2}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134293 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2xyz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}xz[{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}z\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region in the $xz$-plane.\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $x$ and then with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}z\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2z}}{x}^{3}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}z[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-2z}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}z{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{4-2z}}\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(4-2z)^{2}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4z-4{z}^{2}+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[2{z}^{2}-\\frac{4}{3}{z}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{4}]}_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{32}{3}+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131773 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326267 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134294 1071 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134294 1071 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, {x_{1} + x_{2} = 0; 2x_{1} + 2x_{2} = 0$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134294 1074 "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1}{{(-1)}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{2}b \\\\  & =-4b \\\\  & =-4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131779 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326273 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134295 1071 "$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}6x\\,dy]dA,\n$$\n\nEvaluate the repeated integral above given that $D$ is a region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z=0,$ $x=0,$ and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=4.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134295 1071 "$∬_(D)[\\displaystyle \\int_{x^{2}}^{4}6xdy]dA$,\nEvaluate the repeated integral above given that $D$ is a region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z = 0$, $x = 0$, and the curve $z + x^{2} = 4$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134295 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{4}6x\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}6x[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=4}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6x(4-{x}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate this double integral, we plot $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/61/612c4fda39f7003024c322b6d1fa5c279815923905c8d023547a3be6b4a6805a.png)\n\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}24x-6{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(24x-6{x}^{3})[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(24x-6{x}^{3})(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}96x-48{x}^{3}+6{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[48{x}^{2}-12{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(192-192+64)-0 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131791 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326342 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134297 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le z\\le 2{x}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2xz}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134297 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$∭_(R)2xdV$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by\n\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le x \\le 1, x^{2} \\le z \\le 2x^{2}, 0 \\le y \\le 2xz}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134297 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2xz}2x\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2xz}2x\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2xz}2x\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}2x[y]_{y=0}^{y=2xz}dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}2x(2xz)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}4{x}^{2}z\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}4{x}^{2}z\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2{x}^{2}}4{x}^{2}z\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}[{z}^{2}]_{z={x}^{2}}^{z=2{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}((2{x}^{2})^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{6}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{6}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{6{x}^{7}}{7}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131803 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326508 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134299 1071 "$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2x+1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1+x+z}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134299 1071 "$∭_(S)f(x, y, z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}\\displaystyle \\int_{∗}^{∗}f(x, y, z)dydzdx$\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n$S = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le x \\le 1,-1 \\le z \\le 2x + 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1 + x + z}$.\nWhat is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134299 1074 "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)=1+x+z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z\\le 2x+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2x+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2x+1}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=(-1)+(2x+1)=2x.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131815 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326322 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134301 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vectors $v = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134301 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vectors $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134301 1074 "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot (-1)+(-\\sqrt{5})\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 5+1\\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{7}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{\\sqrt{64}\\cdot \\sqrt{32}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{32}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131821 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326230 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134302 1071 "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$\nthat is closest to the vector $x$, where\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134302 1071 "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134302 1074 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131827 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326313 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134303 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y = 2x$ and $y =-x$ from $y = 0$ to $y = 4$. What is $∬_(D)(x^{2} + y^{2})dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134303 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ What is ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134303 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=-x & \\,⟹\\,x=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/df/df512140098a6fc6adc03e4d98199896cf15ed1cdd40578aea14ff1e83dfeba3.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,-y\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y/2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131833 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326454 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134304 1071 "![](images/c7/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x = 2 - y$, $x = \\frac{y}{2}$, and $y = 0$, as shown above, then $∬_(D)xydA =$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134304 1071 "![](images/c7/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=2-y,\\,$ $x=\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134304 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-y & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}y & =-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c9332f8f59e2561af9ba5aa89311263fb772dc987ed0b15d91a6d5eea5cf8e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131863 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326648 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134309 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${A}^{T}=A$\n- ${A}^{2}=A$\n- $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134309 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A^{T} = A$\n2. $A^{2} = A$\n3. $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134309 1074 "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${A}^{T}=A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true since both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131875 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186134311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326421 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186134311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [1759218