true] [17592186152605 1074 "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4({cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326843 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157283 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326844 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155535 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186157774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326581 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157774 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of $R^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $29x_{1}$, where $x_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}$.\n$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186157774 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $29{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157774 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-4-4}{9+16+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n29{x}_{1}=29\\cdot \\frac{7}{29}=7.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155541 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186157775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326627 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157775 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}}$ of $R^{2}$, find $[x]_{B}$ if $x = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186157775 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157775 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}=-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155553 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186157777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326484 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157777 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of $R^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $26x_{1}$, where $x_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}$.\n$B = {\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186157777 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $26{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186157777 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-2}{9+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n26{x}_{1}=26\\cdot \\frac{7}{13}=14.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186166903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326783 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186166904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326786 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186166906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326784 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186171967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326692 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172027 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326693 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186170943 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186172558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326433 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172558 1071 "Solve the inequality $((1 - x)(x - 2))/(x + 5) \\le 0$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186172558 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{(1-x)(x-2)}{x+5}\\le 0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172558 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(1-x)(x-2)}{x+5}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-5.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,1),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,1)$ | $(1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-5,1)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots of $f(x)$ are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in (-5,1]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186170955 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186172560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326319 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172560 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2x^{2} + x - 6}{x} > 0$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186172560 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-6}{x}>0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186172560 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-6}{x}.\n$$\n\nBy factoring its numerator, the function $f(x)$ can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{(x+2)(2x-3)}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Also, $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$ The roots and asymptote divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,\\frac{3}{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals at the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-2,0)$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-2,0)\\cup (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186175934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326684 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186175935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326683 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186175935 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326750 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186175936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326690 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186175936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326752 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186175936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326753 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186175936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326754 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186176483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175006 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186176483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326667 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186176483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186176483 1071 "Determine whether $v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 - 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186176483 1071 "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186176483 1074 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2-2\\te