t have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{1-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(1+\\text{i})}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186176486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175024 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186176486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326283 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186176486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186176486 1071 "Given that $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{1} \\\\ b_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}a_{2} \\\\ b_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{a_{1}a_{2}}{b_{1}b_{2}}$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186176486 1071 "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186176486 1074 "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) &