& =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178618 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186180184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326646 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180184 1071 "![](images/cb/cbd92b205693f1056e4dc2d0fc8154843e62b433b66d9b0ba8918d7f5bb3b8bf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = xe^{y}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^{2} + y^{2} = 4$, traversed from the point $(-2, 0)$ to the point $(0,-2)$, as shown above." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186180184 1071 "![](images/cb/cbd92b205693f1056e4dc2d0fc8154843e62b433b66d9b0ba8918d7f5bb3b8bf.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180184 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{0}^{-2}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{-2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2}-2{e}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178636 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186180187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326631 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180187 1071 "![](images/78/78f8ca3e70afb8377748a18d7024ba297e330add59550a41a87025a0145b56bb.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)9xyds$, where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse $x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 4$ traversed from the point $(2, 0)$ to the point $(0, 1)$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186180187 1071 "![](images/78/78f8ca3e70afb8377748a18d7024ba297e330add59550a41a87025a0145b56bb.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$  traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180187 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}18\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(8-1) \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178642 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186180188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326526 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180188 1071 "Write $3 - \\frac{a + 2}{a - 3}$ as a single fraction." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186180188 1071 "Write $3-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}$ as a single fraction." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180188 1074 "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $a-3$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $a-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{a-3}{a-3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)-(a+2)}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a-9-a-2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a-11}{a-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178648 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186180189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326550 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180189 1071 "Write $4 + \\frac{4}{n}$ as a single fraction." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186180189 1071 "Write $4+\\frac{4}{n}$ as a single fraction." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186180189 1074 "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $n$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{n}{n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4n}{n}+\\frac{4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4n+4}{n} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(n+1)}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326792 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326871 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326794 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184340 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326717 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184340 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326724 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184341 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326722 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183217 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186184773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326649 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184773 1071 "Find the solution of $x^{2} - 2x < 8$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186184773 1071 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}-2x<8.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184773 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & <8 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-8 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2x-8$ lies below the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/83/83e87f616c1e9a74065016df2d6a0bbfac5bcdc8ee9326721b13a2bd8a8f16d4.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x-8<0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-2<x<4.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-2,4).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183223 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186184774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326240 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184774 1071 "Find the solution of $2x^{2} - 12 \\le - 5x$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186184774 1071 "Find the solution of $2{x}^{2}-12\\le -5x.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184774 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-12 & \\le -5x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+5x-12 & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+5x-12$ lies below the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5x-12 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+8x)+(-3x-12) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+4)-3(x+4) & =0 \\\\ (2x-3)(x+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/ba/ba6c384800a5b42f2033527997328aa9f9d792b33b3fabecfe9b3a93789f46c4.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n \n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+5x-12\\le 0,\n$$\n\n we look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\n Based on the graph, the solution is $-4\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in [-4,\\frac{3}{2}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183229 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186184775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326527 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184775 1071 "Find the solution of $-6x^{2} > - 3x$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186184775 1071 "Find the solution of $-6{x}^{2}>-3x.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186184775 1074 "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2} & >-3x \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=-6{x}^{2}+3x$ lies above the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ -3x(2x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/3d/3dff64c74abd4981c69686cbbf7d79fdd49c7a7fde3bb266b2b6e11c441bcf20.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n-6{x}^{2}+3x>0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $0<x<\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326822 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326852 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326740 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198427 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326725 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198454 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326853 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197588 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326250 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198958 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x+y}4z\\,dz]dA\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le x}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198958 1071 "Evaluate the mixed integral\n$∬_(D)[\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x + y}4zdz]dA$\nwhere the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane is given by\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le x}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198958 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{x+y}4z\\,dz]dA & ={∬}_{D}[2{z}^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=x+y}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)^{2}-0)\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the double integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{x}^{2}+4xy+2{y}^{2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{2}y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}(x-0)+2x({x}^{2}-0)+\\frac{2}{3}({x}^{3}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{14}{3}{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}({1}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197606 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326428 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198961 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$∭_(R)30xdV$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type I region given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1 - x, 0 \\le z \\le 1 - x - y}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198961 1071 "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}30x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type I region given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x-y}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198961 1074 "Writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}30x\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x-y}30x\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}[30xz]_{z=0}^{z=1-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x(1-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}30x-30{x}^{2}-30xy\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[30xy-30{x}^{2}y-15x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1-x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}30x(1-x)-30{x}^{2}(1-x)-15x(1-x)^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+15x\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+15x\\,dx & ={[\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{4}-10{x}^{3}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{15}{4}-10+\\frac{15}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197612 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326361 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198962 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y = x^{2}$ and $x = y^{2}$. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $λ(x, y) = 12Mxy$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198962 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $x={y}^{2}.$ Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=12Mxy.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198962 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 12Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}[3x{y}^{4}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{10}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =M(\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9M}{20}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197618 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326266 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198963 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the part of the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} \\le 4$ in the first quadrant. Its mass density at any point is given by $λ(x, y) = \\frac{3}{8}Mx$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198963 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4$ in the first quadrant. Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{8}Mx.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198963 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{3}{8}Mr\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M({\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot ({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}(1-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197636 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326529 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198966 1071 "![](images/96/96b24f85c19f388c152dae265c3794179ebc9e1e6fc06a1fe10f82627b73d29c.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198966 1071 "![](images/96/96b24f85c19f388c152dae265c3794179ebc9e1e6fc06a1fe10f82627b73d29c.png)\n\nChange the order of integration in the integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{(2 - x)/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2 - x - 2y}f(x, y, z)dzdydx$\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y$, then over a region $D$ in the $xz$-plane. The integration region $R$ is shown above." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198966 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${u}_{2}(x,z)$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x)/2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{2-x}{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2-x-2y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}A,$ we write $R$ as a type III region. \n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f08e4fc4d1d2933e9c12fe7df96a1029d66a83c68beff4fa31928cc010554766.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the back boundary and leaves through the front boundary:\n\n- The front boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=2-x-2y\\,⟹\\,y=(2-x-z)/2.\n$$\n\n- The back boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{2-x-z}{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/26/2639dbff9d1d4269abb59ccd3eefa0b246c8d1f183fd8f6ef132a41cc07e345d.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y$ and then over $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x}{\\int }_{0}^{2-x-2y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{(2-x-z)/2}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197648 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326478 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198968 1071 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D = {(x, y): 1 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le x^{2}}$ and has the mass density function $λ(x, y) = 4xy$. Given that the mass of the lamina is $21$ units and its center of mass is located at $(x^(―), y^(―))$, what is the value of $y^(―)$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198968 1071 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=4xy.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $21$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{y}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198968 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot 4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x{[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4{x}^{7}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{8}}{6}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{6}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{85}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{85}{2})}{21}=\\frac{85}{42}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197672 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326255 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198972 1071 "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $y = 4 - x^{2}$ and the $x$-axis for $-2 \\le x \\le 2$. Given that the area of the plate is $\\frac{32}{3}$, find the $y$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198972 1071 "Consider the thin plate $D$ enclosed between the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis for $-2\\le x\\le 2.$ Given that the area of the plate is $\\frac{32}{3},$ find the $y$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198972 1074 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/53/536f542f535513430311b29f43f6fbafb37832d6dfde3b9c81e82237cb011821.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand ${A}_{D}=\\frac{32}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{32}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(16-8{x}^{2}+{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}{[16x-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}[(32-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{32}{5})-(-32+\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{32}{5})] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197690 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326318 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198975 1071 "![](images/8a/8ac5fd57665f292d8a013f47aaf20226944315bb1f74695e895dca40332b28c6.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}e^{z^{3}}dzdydx$.\nThe region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $dydzdx$.*" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198975 1071 "![](images/8a/8ac5fd57665f292d8a013f47aaf20226944315bb1f74695e895dca40332b28c6.png)\n\nEvaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\nThe region of integration is shown above.\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198975 1074 "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le z\\le 1}\n$$\n\nThe region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/30e43d707479d17f45fb74c023d16706ba9308ffc4421cb17a23d7c2ef16fbee.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/44/4429082d4ab0d6fdef75d79b1db02944f0c53741fb0ccf19d8a4ef46a253698f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}\n$$\n\n- The left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a9/a941e1bfabb21561b622080775c3c5389a86cf4150f1fb28dd692a022f607620.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by $z=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n![](images/20/20e339260b0947f92d8fedfa47e899ed53753e03e30c4b816edec06d092d9a01.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{{z}^{3}}{[y]}_{0}^{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{z}^{2}\\,{e}^{{z}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{e}^{{z}^{3}}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{e-1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}[x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197696 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326438 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198976 1071 "$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{π}}\\displaystyle \\int_{z^{2}}^{\\sqrt{π}}z\\cos (\\frac{π}{6} + y^{2})dydzdx =$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $dzdydx$.*" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198976 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n*Hint: Change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$*" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198976 1074 "We cannot integrate the given integral because we cannot find a simple antiderivative of the integrand. So, we change the order of integration.\n\nWe wish to find the limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,y),$ ${u}_{2}(x,y),$ ${v}_{1}(x),$ ${v}_{2}(x),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi },\\,\\,{z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type III region, we first sketch the projection onto the $xz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3bb477b93fb92174307bee87130d9957b135cdafaa4167f78f3d603c4192391d.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where ${z}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](images/cf/cf966e9ec5fbcf11727d46bb3906f05e4b30df4c9bef86a6eab4850b967d7ba0.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $R$ as a type I region and its projection $D$ onto the $xy$-plane as a type I *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $z$-axis and in the direction of increasing $z,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/88b5e19c3e263d160db8d041fd3504b25d4a8a8f7ae8e111cdeb5b153c3cd21f.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the bottom boundary and leaves through the top boundary:\n\n- The top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x,y),$ is\n\n$$\ny={z}^{2}\\,⟺\\,z=\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\n- The bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b7/b748578888fdda878b0d44bb036310644c3437c1922aafd49cab6c165a9cf132.png)\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xy$-plane with the top curve given by $y=\\sqrt{\\pi }.$\n\n![](images/60/60e35d0e77314aaecc60b0eda3c039afa0bdd44d49f68ad39e27c8856a9390e0.png)\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{\\pi }}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2}){[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\frac{y}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+{y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{x}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197708 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326402 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198978 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy+yz+xz\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198978 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}xy + yz + xzdxdydz$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198978 1074 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ and $z$ as constants:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy+yz+xz\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy+yz+xz\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(y+z)x+yz\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y+z)\\,{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}+yz[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(y+z)(2-0)+yz(2-0)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$, treating $z$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(y+z)+2yz\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}2(1+z)y+2z\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}((1+z)[{y}^{2}]_{1}^{2}+2z[y]_{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}((1+z)(4-1)+2z(2-1))\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3(1+z)+2z)\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3+5z)\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3+5z)\\text{d}z & ={[3z+\\frac{5}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3(1-0)+\\frac{5}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197720 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326252 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198980 1071 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2,-x \\le y \\le x}$. Given that the area of the lamina is $4$ square units, find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198980 1071 "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-x\\le y\\le x}.$ Given that the area of the lamina is $4$ square units, find the $x$-coordinate of its centroid." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198980 1074 "The centroid of a lamina $D$ with area ${A}_{D}$ is given by $({x}_{c},{y}_{c}),$ where\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\,\\text{d}A,\\,{y}_{c}=\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinate of the centroid is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{c} & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{\\int }_{-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x[y]_{y=-x}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197726 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326249 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198981 1071 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}$ and is enclosed between the curve $x=1-{y}^{2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{4}{15}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198981 1071 "Consider a thin plate $D$ that has the mass density function $λ(x, y) = y^{2}$ and is enclosed between the curve $x = 1 - y^{2}$ and the $y$-axis for $-1 \\le y \\le 1$. Given that the mass of the plate is $\\frac{4}{15}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(x^(―), y^(―))$, what is the value of $x^(―)$?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198981 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/78/78f9da3d48727d2cb3c447be0f4050a044c4aabe29179fd31f04cd7b6c66eabd.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1-{y}^{2},\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)={y}^{2}.$ \n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot (1-2{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}-2{y}^{4}+{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{2}{5}{y}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{y}^{7}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{105}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{8}{105})}{(\\frac{4}{15})}=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197750 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326366 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198985 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x) \\cdot Q(y)$, where $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533422 17592186045585 false] [17592186198985 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186198985 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186323983 1081 "17" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186323983 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186323983 1084 #uuid "00008a5f-46bf-51bc-bc71-7e6e5690142d" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186323984 1081 "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}x\\,dy\\,dx" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186323984 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186323984 1084 #uuid "003753be-91cd-5401-b185-1a2caa7b0ba9" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186323985 1081 "(-\\frac{1}{2},