86326857 1074 "Using the formula  \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-4)+(-2)\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{25}\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{6}{25})≈{76.11}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326590 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326858 1069 "q-40565" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326858 1071 "Find the acute angle between the vectors\n $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ \\text{ }4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326858 1072 #uuid "695bf8dc-adcc-5ba6-b927-749766914d40" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326858 1074 "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}\\cdot (-\\sqrt{7})+7\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 4}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}+{7}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{7})^{2}+{3}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{64}\\sqrt{81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326406 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326859 1069 "q-40566" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326859 1071 "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors  $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\text{ }1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326859 1072 #uuid "ca41f5b8-b3cf-55a5-b73c-3a463b4f4955" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326859 1074 "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-5)+(-4)\\cdot (-5)+5\\cdot 2+(-5)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-5)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{67}\\sqrt{55}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3685}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3685}})≈{70.76}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326602 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326860 1069 "q-40571" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326860 1071 "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326860 1072 #uuid "856e08d5-8df4-53f3-8efe-22097e92c2b8" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326860 1074 "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}27 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326305 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326861 1069 "q-40573" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326861 1071 "Find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane  $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326861 1072 #uuid "0c985ebf-e974-5d23-b225-4f6255e4f5ca" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326861 1074 "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326568 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326862 1069 "q-40577" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326862 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{15}{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{n-1}$ ?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326862 1072 #uuid "f208d59b-bccf-5fd6-a6b7-a683ef46db60" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326862 1074 "The series starts with $n=3.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{15}{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{4-1}}{\\frac{2}{15}{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{3-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{3}}{{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326405 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326863 1069 "q-40581" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326863 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x-2,$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ above the $x$-axis. What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326863 1072 #uuid "3786eeaa-4a33-58cb-9504-23515a98f21e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326863 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/68/6817e13f2f4c019f337425bd9f2284386c7077c0ac97da51fc26fdea19f8ee67.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x-2$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=y+2 \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =y+2 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/db/db9d1846b19b75a629ad0a0d4b6e138aa2cef91cd8c0324a9285ed9c44befb60.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le y+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y+2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326668 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326864 1069 "q-40590" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326864 1071 "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}}$ converge?" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326864 1072 #uuid "342a1074-4db1-5f41-b2c0-7147d022edf7" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326864 1074 "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn+1}}{{4}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{16}^{kn}\\cdot 16}{{4}^{n}\\cdot {4}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(16\\cdot 4){(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }64{(\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{16}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {16}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{16}^{k}}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${16}^{k}=4$ has the solution $k=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Now, since ${16}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${16}^{k}<4$ must be $k<\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326280 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326865 1069 "q-40591" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326865 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326865 1072 #uuid "532e9715-de59-568c-a5cc-48ba89edb532" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326865 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Computing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)=0.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The vector $x$ can be written as $x={a}_{1}-{a}_{2}.$ So, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ Therefore, the projection of $x$ onto $S$ is $x$ itself and the angle between $x$ and the subspace is equal to $0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326594 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326866 1069 "q-40592" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326866 1071 "Solve the inequality $(x+5)(x+3)(x-2)<0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326866 1072 #uuid "b6e4d222-8e80-58d0-970e-c5a7b4af4440" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326866 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+5)(x+3)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-3),\\,(-3,2),\\,(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-3)$ | $(-3,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(-3,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5)\\cup (-3,2).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326867 1069 "q-40605" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326867 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326867 1072 #uuid "607e5216-1caa-5fc1-a284-7e9f9d470560" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326867 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-3)^{2}+(0-0)^{2}+(-3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326645 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326868 1069 "q-40610" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326868 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326868 1072 #uuid "c22b52e6-cf75-58b2-823a-b76f6234d620" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326868 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\