s, $x$ lies in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326558 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326871 1069 "q-40619" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326871 1071 "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326871 1072 #uuid "c9d8685d-2896-58f6-9f97-cb8ee658ca47" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326871 1074 "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326308 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326872 1069 "q-40637" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326872 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}\\le 0.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326872 1072 #uuid "0b5f0db2-f1c3-5d85-b6a3-492b0666d0fd" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326872 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x-15) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-3x+5x-15) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x-3)+5(x-3)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+5)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+5)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-5,0)$ and $(0,3).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-5,3].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326238 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326873 1069 "q-40647" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326873 1071 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $r=sv+tw$, where\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326873 1072 #uuid "d982cd02-c41a-5998-91ea-b4d59641d0b2" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326873 1074 "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}{v,w}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begi