sect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis and below the line $y=1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/37/373286c03cc595535a7f04987e7454be2fac547d769341343f05ea67ee0b001d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{[\\,\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y\\,]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4-{y}^{2})ydy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4y-{y}^{3})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4y-{y}^{3})dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326672 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326878 1069 "q-406820" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326878 1071 "![](images/31/31db08aa86f46d6601820b34dd938bc4b3738cba90f2fdd2721b4eb2069fa40b.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$, the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$, 