2186326881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326385 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326881 1069 "q-40747" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326881 1071 "![](images/ce/ce364dc7c6c4fc5e229fdeb0809b3779bf1052c60d956deb742731f34e54562d.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=2x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=4,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dA=$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326881 1072 #uuid "f8348ba1-d0ae-5fbd-ab2d-88304b94eb8a" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326881 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/54/54f366679684823341bab818459ce5fc884ebbdd2376e800b781d1b832c4ba64.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2x\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2x}^{4}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326281 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326882 1069 "q-40756" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326882 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,12).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326882 1072 #uuid "ea730d8c-46db-5efe-9ba8-729d43d49a6b" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326882 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326502 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326883 1069 "q-40761" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326883 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-k,k),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326883 1072 #uuid "74d79016-8845-5f4d-914d-8daddb3b0e8e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326883 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-k)^{2}+{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-k}{k})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326299 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326884 1069 "q-40762" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326884 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{k}{4},\\frac{k}{2}),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer where necessary." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326884 1072 #uuid "9cbc72bf-acb2-5746-a596-97d89687c315" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326884 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{k}{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{k}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{k}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{k}^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5\\cdot \\frac{{k}^{2}}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{(\\frac{k}{2})}{(-\\frac{k}{4})})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-2)| \\\\  & ≈1.107.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -1.107 \\\\  & ≈2.035 \\\\  & ≈2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}\\,k}{4},2).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326294 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326885 1069 "q-40798" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326885 1071 "Which of the following values ​are possible outputs of  $f(x)={(1.5)}^{2x}-0.5?$ \n\n- $0$\n- $-0.2$\n- $-0.8$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326885 1072 #uuid "f9f596e3-81e5-5b3c-9173-334b5d50904e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326885 1074 "The range of ${(1.5)}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{(1.5)}^{x}>0.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches do not affect the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{(1.5)}^{2x}>0.\n$$\n\nSubtracting  $0.5$ from the above inequality yields the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1.5)}^{2x} & >0 \\\\ {(1.5)}^{2x}-0.5 & >0-0.5 \\\\ f(x) & >-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)>-0.5.$ Thus, of the given values, only $y=0$ and $y=-0.2$ are outputs of $y=f(x).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/58/5876d2095bfd91417d2e6395f8a02ae817ebd476076bae929d79709ea7961a3e.png)\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326297 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326886 1069 "q-40799" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326886 1071 "Which of the following values ​are possible outputs of $f(x)=-{(2)}^{-x}?$\n\n- $0$\n- $0.5$\n- $-0.5$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326886 1072 #uuid "5188349b-4c96-553c-a9c2-bb2336ff508e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326886 1074 "The range of ${2}^{-x}$ is\n\n$$\n{2}^{-x}>0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-1$ yields the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1){(2)}^{-x} & <0 \\\\ -{(2)}^{-x} & <0 \\\\ f(x) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)<0.$ Thus, of the given values, only $y=-0.5$ is an output of $y=f(x).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95ad3043259402375e7b5822aef88d77027222a3372c2b8ab042d1b54e08666f.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326607 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326887 1069 "q-40804" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326887 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326887 1072 #uuid "351973f9-e651-502b-90b1-ba68a5d4bedc" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326887 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{10})^{2},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326226 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326888 1069 "q-40822" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326888 1071 "![](images/93/933e68e42fe61af3e30fae012b0b56dce6793b0fadc343e04a0766b85cb6ae7e.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=9-{x}^{2}$ and $y=1+{x}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dA=$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326888 1072 #uuid "74e8d822-2417-5c6e-b5f9-e86873e83a1b" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326888 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{x}^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/c5/c56c5be4ac88755f1859f295f700be8710d75b16380ac120e6b56a3aa767acbe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,1+{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 9-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1+{x}^{2}}^{9-{x}^{2}}(x{y}^{2}+2x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326500 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326889 1069 "q-40825" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326889 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $y=\\sqrt{x},$ and $x=4,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326889 1072 #uuid "1c444980-62b8-5433-81a1-2731b5e581cf" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326889 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/fc/fcb347767983c38eb12f521ca556dcc5a96705ee4e887fb8d43c42984dfe1a26.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x\\sqrt{x} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{x})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/91/91bf7087e5aa0053d24cb45158d48b0bb36fb84b28ebabaeba3c26dc62168dfe.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1/x}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326314 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326890 1069 "q-40826" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326890 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,-1).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326890 1072 #uuid "417c4b2b-c91e-563b-b92c-41495ea7b2b8" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326890 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(8,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(8,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326891 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326464 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326891 1069 "q-40830" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326891 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326891 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326891 1072 #uuid "6d802aac-a480-5058-8cd4-2001f10175cc" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326891 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{15}=\\frac{4\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/84/845121f81ff0dbe29a073319fbdeeab86e1c8b145471e8d3ec655d49d05d86cd.png)" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326892 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326306 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326892 1069 "q-40882" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326892 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326892 1071 "Consider the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{k} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u\\cdot v=-20$ and $‖v‖=5$,  what is the smallest possible value of $k?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326892 1072 #uuid "0ea0a813-a7a6-50d9-bc45-5213ad249e0e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326892 1074 "We will use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality,\n\n$$\n|u\\cdot v|\\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖.\n$$\n\nWe are given all the quantities in the inequality above, except for $‖u‖.$ So first, we compute $‖u‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{k})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14+k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖\\cdot ‖v‖ \\\\ |-20| & \\le \\sqrt{14+k}\\cdot (5) \\\\ 4 & \\le \\sqrt{14+k} \\\\ {4}^{2} & \\le {(\\sqrt{14+k})}^{2} \\\\ 16 & \\le 14+k \\\\ 2 & \\le k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $k$ is $2.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326370 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326893 1069 "q-40884" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326893 1071 "Rounded to two decimal places, what is the acute angle between the vectors $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326893 1072 #uuid "ac040723-2ada-5099-94bc-dbb9317cacda" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326893 1074 "Using the formula\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖},\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(1)+(0)(1)+(1)(1)+(2)(1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})≈{33.56}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326669 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326894 1069 "q-40887" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326894 1071 "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326894 1072 #uuid "c5b28021-7745-5804-91ca-f5acfea13aef" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326894 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{45}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326380 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326895 1069 "q-40898" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326895 1071 "Given that $‖6u+v‖=11$ and $‖v‖=2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326895 1072 #uuid "d6e826f5-1082-581e-bc2e-f5f57cf8a6a6" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326895 1074 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖6u+v‖ & \\le ‖6u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖6u+v‖ & \\le 6‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 11 & \\le 6‖u‖+2 \\\\ 9 & \\le 6‖u‖ \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326521 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326896 1069 "q-40899" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326896 1071 "Given that $‖4u+v‖=29$ and $‖v‖=5$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326896 1072 #uuid "b936df0a-3fc4-5da3-967c-98a650d14f38" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326896 1074 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le ‖4u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖4u+v‖ & \\le 4‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 29 & \\le 4‖u‖+5 \\\\ 24 & \\le 4‖u‖ \\\\ 6 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $6.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326339 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326897 1069 "q-40901" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326897 1071 "Given that $‖9u+v‖=23$ and $‖v‖=5$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326897 1072 #uuid "674fea9a-ccf5-5e63-98a8-86814d04a430" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326897 1074 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖9u+v‖ & \\le ‖9u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖9u+v‖ & \\le 9‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 23 & \\le 9‖u‖+5 \\\\ 18 & \\le 9‖u‖ \\\\ 2 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $2.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326275 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326898 1069 "q-40903" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326898 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326898 1072 #uuid "9d95e69c-d8b6-504a-9a9e-0e0569042175" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326898 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $3$ and then adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le 1 \\\\  & -3\\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le 3 \\\\  & -1\\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2\\le 5 \\\\  & -1\\le f(x)\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $f(x)\\in [-1,5].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326545 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326899 1069 "q-40913" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326899 1071 "![](images/19/19f94b8985a4b8da7899f984e0e1c3f88668aa59531658cd0118edb35aab1107.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326899 1072 #uuid "1c8c8181-1284-56b6-82a4-ada1681baa78" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326899 1074 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{-6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326378 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326900 1069 "q-40921" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326900 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326900 1072 #uuid "d34cdae0-7b23-59fc-a8bc-f0d99444106f" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326900 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ -18 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{2}{9}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326447 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326901 1069 "q-40923" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326901 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326901 1072 #uuid "c3df77ba-fc93-5eb8-9827-0c8cef3d9027" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326901 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-27 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ -30 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}=-10\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=5.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326434 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326902 1069 "q-40927" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326902 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326902 1072 #uuid "5b6fc54b-cb36-5749-9df6-d5883bd36fd6" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326902 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-2a+\\frac{b}{c}=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=3.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326395 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326903 1069 "q-40950" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326903 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a-3b-6c?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326903 1072 #uuid "db567127-1e8b-50cb-a3d2-dc5e2ea462cd" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326903 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(1/6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(1/2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable.  \n\nFrom the third row, we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=1,\n$$\n\nand from the first row we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a-3b-6c=2(\\frac{1}{2})-3(1)-6(0)=-2.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326589 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326904 1069 "q-41032" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326904 1071 "$$\nV=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326904 1072 #uuid "fa171dfe-33fe-5987-b734-14774b114908" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326904 1074 "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{5\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $b=2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326532 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326905 1069 "q-41034" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326905 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326905 1072 #uuid "4c9da90f-19cf-5646-82a3-18682358ed1b" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326905 1074 "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+0\\cdot \\sqrt{3}+7\\cdot 0}{{3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{0}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-\\sqrt{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{1}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326674 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326906 1069 "q-41106" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326906 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326906 1072 #uuid "a89fb0a0-3a36-552f-b729-6c5581cebcd4" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326906 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot 3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326610 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326907 1069 "q-41253" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326907 1071 "Find Cartesian coordinates of the point of intersection of the polar curve $r=5$ with the positive $x$-axis." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326907 1072 #uuid "cd9bcab3-a034-50a1-983b-c578936ecb86" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326907 1074 "The polar curve $r=5$ is a circle of radius $5$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $x$-axis at the point $(5,0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/54/54e01fed14e44783dd13a1d7163688ab4e2c788997697dd3ae26fe8e00dac47d.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(5,0).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326377 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326908 1069 "q-41257" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326908 1071 "Find the polar equation of the circle that is centered at the origin and that passes through the point $A(-5,-3).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326908 1072 #uuid "67d7a8a0-c18c-502f-83e4-7b8f23a432d9" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326908 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{34}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326539 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326909 1069 "q-41467" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326909 1071 "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 168\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326909 1072 #uuid "3832f676-5ec9-5b13-afe4-f7324769e216" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326909 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 168\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4.2 \\\\ 6.8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-4.2+6.8x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the value of the sales when the advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.2+6.8(5) \\\\  & =29.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the total sales when the advertising budget equals $$5$ million will be $$29.8$ million. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=5$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326259 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326910 1069 "q-41626" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326910 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326910 1072 #uuid "b904a59c-d936-5bd3-ab5c-5deda9350aee" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326910 1074 "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{4}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{6}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{5})}{(-\\frac{1}{5})}=2.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326659 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326911 1069 "q-41630" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326911 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\text{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ of the matrix $A,$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ shown above. Given that ${a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|\\,a\\,|?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326911 1072 #uuid "73028088-3653-5062-a918-602555877417" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326911 1074 "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{10}{10})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(\\frac{0}{2})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${v}_{3}$ dividing it by $‖{v}_{3}‖=3\\sqrt{10}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326576 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326912 1069 "q-41702" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326912 1071 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the $x$-axis, for $\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi .$ Express ${\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326912 1072 #uuid "dfeb76a7-b3b4-5da5-8979-e2b8f3b5d4af" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326912 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/264cb888010525848f647669653d34383535dd32e6ac264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326551 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326913 1069 "q-41738" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326913 1071 "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $-3$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326913 1072 #uuid "dab2f9a8-5f69-5399-b09f-fee3c3a21d8e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326913 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector. \n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3-2.5x.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326288 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326914 1069 "q-41776" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326914 1071 "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326914 1072 #uuid "082f22b9-7dbc-5c7c-88d0-775a3d957e87" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326914 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326352 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326915 1069 "q-41780" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326915 1071 "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326915 1072 #uuid "b2442a57-a3cd-5f70-ab1c-b200915b57c3" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326915 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 29\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}29 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326372 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326916 1069 "q-41787" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326916 1071 "![](images/7a/7a3c82568cfaf845708a9dcee79a3ac2e0e9d2131776fd1c7daa1c598473f3b3.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=10$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326916 1072 #uuid "1aff9346-7008-5138-9f81-65abfd7bc8f5" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326916 1074 "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(10,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-5\\sqrt{3},-5).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326382 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326917 1069 "q-41811" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326917 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+2{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,0)$ and $(1,1).$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326917 1072 #uuid "63bc1464-04bd-5198-8418-9e8745d1335c" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326917 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326373 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326918 1069 "q-41840" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326918 1071 "What is the range of the function $y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})+4?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326918 1072 #uuid "8be6063d-ee1d-5aea-9fe3-f1f6572dff56" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326918 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and then adding $4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\ge -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})\\ge -2 \\\\ 6 & \\ge -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{30}^{∘})+4\\ge 2 \\\\ 2 & \\le y\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [2,6].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326538 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326919 1069 "q-41904" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326919 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $15a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326919 1072 #uuid "85d7c223-d931-53ec-98fb-80a06e5b2764" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326919 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n15a+\\frac{b}{c}=15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=2.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326298 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326920 1069 "q-41915" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326920 1071 "Find the value of $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{⌊1-x⌋}}{x}$ at $x=-1.5.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326920 1072 #uuid "6737dfbb-c4f2-5367-b0f6-611f227909d5" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326920 1074 "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $⌊x⌋$ equals the greatest integer that is less than or equal to $x:$\n\n$$\n⌊x⌋=n,\\,\\text{where}\\,n\\le x<n+1,\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=-1.5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.5) & =\\frac{{e}^{⌊1-(-1.5)⌋}}{(-1.5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{⌊1+1.5⌋}}{1.5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{⌊2.5⌋}}{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function, we have\n\n$$\n⌊2.5⌋=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.5) & =-\\frac{{e}^{⌊2.5⌋}}{1.5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{2}}{1.5} \\\\  & =-4.926\\,03… \\\\  & ≈-4.93,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326387 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326921 1069 "q-41916" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326921 1071 "Find the value of $f(x)=\\frac{⌊{x}^{3}⌋}{{x}^{2}}$ at $x=-2.5.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326921 1072 #uuid "4a4b6819-14e2-5d5a-bb0b-e28da4533edf" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326921 1074 "Suppose $x$ is a real number. The greatest integer (or floor) function $⌊x⌋$ equals the greatest integer that is less than or equal to $x:$\n\n$$\n⌊x⌋=n,\\,\\text{where}\\,n\\le x<n+1,\\,n\\in Z.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(x)$ at $x=-2.5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.5) & =\\frac{⌊(-2.5)^{3}⌋}{(-2.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⌊-15.625⌋}{6.25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the greatest integer function, we have\n\n$$\n⌊-15.625⌋=-16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.5) & =\\frac{⌊-15.625⌋}{6.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-16)}{6.25} \\\\  & =-2.56.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326419 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326922 1069 "q-41944" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326922 1071 "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326922 1072 #uuid "2203e95e-8ae6-5711-8426-24e1b1e66cba" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326922 1074 "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\sqrt{2} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326673 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326923 1069 "q-41964" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326923 1071 "What is the range of the function $y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})+1?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326923 1072 #uuid "87e222c7-c912-54d0-9c4f-f1a9126f53ea" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326923 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-4$ and then adding $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})\\le 1 \\\\  & 4\\ge -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})\\ge -4 \\\\  & 5\\ge -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})+1\\ge -3 \\\\  & -3\\le y\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [-3,5].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326608 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326924 1069 "q-41977" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326924 1071 "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326924 1072 #uuid "2c865f40-7da0-527e-98f7-3ab8537abeb4" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326924 1074 "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326653 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326925 1069 "q-41979" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326925 1071 "Find the product $AB$, if\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{2} & 1.5 & 2 \\\\ -3.5 & -2\\sqrt{3} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326925 1072 #uuid "fc71896c-5daf-5e26-9560-8339a3f927a3" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326925 1074 "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}-3.5 & -2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{2} & 1.5 & 2 \\\\ -3.5 & -2\\sqrt{3} & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3.5 & -2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326554 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326926 1069 "q-42001" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326926 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326926 1072 #uuid "0594dca0-de16-5429-abef-c4b9b45aec7e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326926 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $3$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 42 & 0 & -21 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326619 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326927 1069 "q-42002" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326927 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326927 1072 #uuid "872a04d1-c3a7-5abb-ba35-c6553a0edf68" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326927 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $1\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding only $1$ entry.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 24 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326928 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326540 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326928 1069 "q-42014" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326928 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326928 1071 "$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\\,,[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nSuppose that $V=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ Given that $B$ is an **orthonormal** basis for $V,{a}_{1}⊥{a}_{2},$ and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|\\,b\\,|?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326928 1072 #uuid "2ec15ecd-6f69-516e-9bcc-05f59ef19d6e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326928 1074 "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{10}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{25}{5}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${v}_{3}$ dividing it by $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{10}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,b\\,|=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326498 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326929 1069 "q-42028" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326929 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 11\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326929 1072 #uuid "324c0e93-0bc4-5958-ba8a-b87ad1b81b6a" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326929 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the bottom of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column to the right of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since ${a}_{32}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated). \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the first row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is in row echelon form." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326359 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326930 1069 "q-42041" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326930 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4z=2 \\\\ 4x+y-5z=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326930 1072 #uuid "a7ff9f1a-cb7f-5669-aa2d-6b7ae8d5b3bd" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326930 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2z=1 \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+2z \\\\ y=-3z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326256 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326931 1069 "q-42048" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326931 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨k,2,2⟩$, $b=⟨3,0,2⟩$ and $c=⟨4,1,3⟩$ are coplanar." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326931 1072 #uuid "6db83569-7cb0-5537-ab90-423f6f08147e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326931 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)-2(3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 2)+2(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-2k-2+6 \\\\  & =-2k+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ -2k+4 & =0 \\\\ 2k & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=2.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326595 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326932 1069 "q-42050" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326932 1071 "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $a=⟨1,-2,1⟩$, $b=⟨2,-4,1⟩$ and $c=⟨0,2,5⟩.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326932 1072 #uuid "cf69007e-3a95-53fc-ae94-e7b4176da553" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326932 1074 "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|-(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 1)+2(2\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 1)+(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =-22+20+4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|2|=2.\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326245 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326933 1069 "q-42103" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326933 1071 "$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|=$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326933 1072 #uuid "c9f42611-c6f2-56b0-8496-f724b3d11a6e" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326933 1074 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{13}+1\\cdot {C}_{23}+0\\cdot {C}_{33}+0\\cdot {C}_{43} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326354 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326934 1069 "q-42107" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326934 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326934 1072 #uuid "977cc01a-2db6-51fc-a3f5-772fb936cc60" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326934 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326661 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326935 1069 "q-42116" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326935 1071 "Consider the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is lower triangular\n- ${A}^{T}-A$ is lower triangular\n- $A$ is upper triangular" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326935 1072 #uuid "7fc84187-05b2-57ce-8e48-261250d11c80" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326935 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, since the result of ${A}^{T}-A$ has non-zero entries above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false, since $A$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326228 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326936 1069 "q-42123" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326936 1071 "A partitioned matrix $M$ contains the following blocks: \n\n- ${M}_{11},$ which is a $2\\times 3$ block\n\n- ${M}_{12},$ which is a $2\\times 2$ block\n\n- ${M}_{22},$ which is a $1\\times 2$ block\n\nWhat are the dimensions of ${M}_{21}?$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326936 1072 #uuid "3b9e0d32-b5dc-57e1-95b6-1cc48988ca5f" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326936 1074 "We have a matrix of the form\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & {M}_{12} \\\\ {M}_{21} & {M}_{22}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\times 3 & 2\\times 2 \\\\ ?\\times ? & 1\\times 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${M}_{21}$ must be a $1\\times 3$ block." 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326415 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326937 1069 "q-42127" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326937 1071 "Which of the following matrices are block lower triangular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326937 1072 #uuid "366fb16a-48e3-5635-add3-b8c862272224" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326937 1074 "A block lower triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks above the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $B$ is a block lower triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* a block lower triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $B$ only.\"" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326325 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326938 1069 "q-42129" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326938 1071 "Find \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & b & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|$\ngiven that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & 2 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}|=-5.$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326938 1072 #uuid "cc08f308-2d18-506d-9143-fe2b2d3a192c" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true] [17592186326938 1074 "Notice that we have  a block upper triangular matrix. Therefore, its determinant is equal to the product of the diagonal blocks' determinants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & b & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a & 2 & 10 \\\\ 0 & b & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}a & 2 \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}|\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533422 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-00008a5f-46bf-51bc-bc71-7e6e5690142d" 17592186323983 "answer-003753be-91cd-5401-b185-1a2caa7b0ba9" 17592186323984 "answer-00cc317f-9860-521f-8e35-82b0b96775d5" 17592186323985 "answer-00e93ff9-4fa0-56f0-9956-482957cd7626" 17592186323986 "answer-010d84a7-1ce6-5b20-8fb6-ef470203e66a" 17592186323987 "answer-012f43b3-ef0b-5283-ab39-5b15604561cc" 17592186323988 "answer-015e6485-5574-5b4c-958f-69b35790a368" 17592186323989 "answer-016c1c57-aef5-5671-beb7-b0e827de3e7c" 17592186323990 "answer-017114c3-a1a2-5d5e-aec5-f7291e21000d" 17592186323991 "answer-0190a5f0-08d1-5ff7-acf6-27f699845af6" 17592186323992 "answer-019501e8-6ea6-5368-99e8-cde9c215f7d2" 17592186323993 "answer-01c262fc-9bb7-50e9-9bd9-71220d222eee" 17592186323994 "answer-01fcdf7a-1068-52e4-936a-c57780fe74d0" 17592186323995 "answer-0212ab39-d874-5d7c-ad86-c2241618470a" 17592186323996 "answer-02310999-31d5-55c2-910f-da601b7a00ff" 17592186323997 "answer-0233521b-a096-5594-a86d-c130ae9967d1" 17592186323998 "answer-026fe2ca-9cd9-566e-b3e9-6fad3f3ad4df" 17592186323999 "answer-027310f9-ee0d-507a-bc28-d9da862bc3bf" 17592186324000 "answer-029cbf11-ac86-5d19-84c3-980a75b99403" 17592186324001 "answer-02a3420d-46e5-5ca9-a685-0dbf5933828d" 17592186324002 "answer-02a9e964-e971-5a63-8ddf-d35611c16d45" 17592186324003 "answer-02ba46e1-9fb1-54f5-9f26-4a03d84e709f" 17592186324004 "answer-02ec0914-5828-5c4a-81c9-ce52c1488ee6" 17592186324005 "answer-0303d738-8cc7-59ec-8b7d-200d38ab8c78" 17592186324006 "answer-03088fb9-3740-556c-b57c-741692e31e1e" 17592186324007 "answer-03126d12-5d9f-529e-83a9-895db4dfa19b" 17592186324008 "answer-031311d8-4f06-51fe-89d1-8d5c2d6815da" 17592186324009 "answer-03307e39-9912-54d0-97d1-4687514485a1" 17592186324010 "answer-034fdef5-1627-5580-b26d-5b7fc920737f" 17592186324011 "answer-0368bac0-98